            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The ship primarily symbolizes the Church navigating the perilous waters of the world to bring believers safely to salvation. Secondary meanings include the individual believer's spiritual journey and the worldly commerce, wealth, and pride of nations like Tyre and Babylon.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A ship symbolizes an organized kingdom, city, or state supported by the 'sea' of peoples, governed by its rulers (steerers) and citizens (crew).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The Church (primary); worldly commerce and wealth (secondary).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"An organized kingdom, city, or state.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus identifies the ship primarily as a spiritual vessel (the Church or the soul), while the book redefines it as a corporate or political entity (a kingdom, city, or state).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book rejects the 'commonly taught' view that the ship inherently symbolizes the Church. Instead, it asserts that the ship is a human political structure, which only comes under Christ's headship if He actively 'boards' it.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book explicitly shifts the primary meaning away from the Church to a socio-political entity (a state or city). While the consensus acknowledges Tyre and Babylon in the context of commerce, the book universalizes the ship as the structural organization of any kingdom or state, fundamentally contradicting the traditional ecclesiological consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While Ezekiel 27 perfectly supports the allegory of a city-state as a ship, the Gospel accounts of Jesus and the disciples in boats are much more naturally read as representing the nascent Christian community (the Church) rather than a 'kingdom, city, or state'.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
